<span class="kicker">2026-07-20 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>Do Professionals Seal Cracks in Driveways or Patios</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Yes, professionals do seal cracks in driveways and patios, but it’s important to know this upfront: sealing is usually a temporary fix, not a permanent solution. It can slow down further damage and buy time, but if the crack came from a deeper issue, like an aging surface or a poorly built base, sealing alone won’t stop it from coming back.</p>
<p>At<a href="/about/"> Ridgewell Landscape and Design</a>, crack sealing and repair is part of what we do across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and surrounding communities. Here’s an honest look at when sealing makes sense, when it doesn’t, and what actually solves the problem long-term.</p>
<h2><b>The Short Answer</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Question</b></td><td><b>Answer</b></td></tr><tr><td>Do professionals seal cracks?</td><td>Yes, it’s a standard service</td></tr><tr><td>Is sealing a permanent fix?</td><td>No, it’s more of a stopgap</td></tr><tr><td>Most common causes of cracks?</td><td>Age/normal wear and poor original base/installation</td></tr><tr><td>What actually solves the problem?</td><td>Addressing the base or replacing the damaged section</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><b>Why Cracks Happen in the First Place</b></h2>
<p>Before jumping into sealing, it helps to understand why cracks show up. In our experience, two causes come up more than anything else.</p>
<p><b>Age and normal wear.</b> Every driveway and patio has a lifespan. Years of temperature swings, sun exposure, and everyday use gradually wear down the surface, and cracking is often just a sign that a surface is reaching the end of its usable life.</p>
<p><b>Poor original base or installation.</b> This is the bigger issue, because it means the cracking isn’t really about the surface material at all, it’s about what’s underneath. A driveway or patio built on a shallow, poorly compacted base is far more likely to crack early, regardless of how nice the concrete, stone, or pavers on top look. This is why getting<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> proper base depth</a> right from the start matters so much, whether you’re building a new patio or a driveway.</p>

<h2>Turn Your dream landscape a reality</h2>

