</a></p>

<h2><b>What Sealing Actually Does</b></h2>
<p>Sealing a crack does exactly what it sounds like: it fills the gap and creates a barrier against water and debris. That barrier matters because water getting into a crack is what turns a small problem into a bigger one, especially with Colorado’s freeze-thaw cycles, where water expands as it freezes and widens the crack from the inside.</p>
<p>So sealing isn’t pointless. It genuinely slows down deterioration and protects the surrounding area from getting worse. What it doesn’t do is fix whatever caused the crack to form in the first place.</p>
<h2><b>Why Sealing Alone Isn’t a Permanent Fix</b></h2>
<p>Here’s the honest reality: a sealed crack is still a crack. If the underlying cause was a weak base, ongoing settling, or age-related wear, that same stress is still there after sealing, the crack is just temporarily covered. Over time, especially through another freeze-thaw season, it’s common for a sealed crack to reopen or for new cracks to form nearby.</p>
<p>This is why we’re upfront with clients: sealing buys time and prevents faster damage, but it’s not the same as solving the problem.</p>
<h2><b>When Sealing Makes Sense vs. When It Doesn’t</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Situation</b></td><td><b>Best Approach</b></td></tr><tr><td>Small, surface-level crack on an otherwise solid base</td><td>Sealing works well as a protective measure</td></tr><tr><td>Cracking spread across a large area</td><td>Points to a base issue, sealing won’t hold</td></tr><tr><td>Crack keeps reappearing after sealing</td><td>Underlying base likely needs repair</td></tr><tr><td>Surface is old and worn overall</td><td>Sealing is a short-term patch, not a fix</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If a crack is isolated and the rest of the surface is in good shape, sealing is a smart, low-cost way to protect it. If cracks are widespread or keep coming back, that’s usually a sign the base needs attention, not just the surface.</p>
<h2><b>What a Real Fix Looks Like</b></h2>
<p>When sealing isn’t enough, the actual solution usually involves one of two things:</p>
<ul><li><b>Base repair</b>, addressing the compaction, drainage, or grading issue that’s causing pressure on the surface from underneath</li><li><b>Section replacement</b>, removing and rebuilding the damaged area properly, rather than patching over it</li></ul>
<p>Neither of these is as quick or cheap as sealing, which is exactly why sealing gets recommended so often, it’s the fastest, most affordable option. But it’s worth knowing the difference between “this will hold for a while” and “this actually solves the problem.” In many cases, a section that’s failed repeatedly is a good candidate for a full<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio installation</a> rather than another repair cycle, since pavers handle Colorado’s ground movement better than a single poured slab. You can read more about why in our guide on<a href="/blog/what-is-hardscaping/"> what hardscaping actually means</a> and how it holds up over time.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>Is crack sealing worth doing if it’s not permanent?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, in the right situation. Sealing slows water damage and protects a crack from getting worse, which can meaningfully extend the life of a driveway or patio, even if it’s not a permanent fix.</p>
<h3><b>How do I know if my crack needs sealing or full repair?</b></h3>
<p>A small, isolated crack on an otherwise solid surface is usually fine with sealing. Widespread cracking, sinking, or cracks that keep coming back point to a base issue that needs real repair.</p>
<h3><b>Why do cracks keep coming back after sealing?</b></h3>
<p>Because sealing addresses the crack itself, not the cause. If the base underneath is weak or poorly installed, the same pressure that caused the first crack is still there.</p>
<h3><b>Does Colorado’s climate make cracking worse?</b></h3>
<p>Yes. Freeze-thaw cycles push water into small cracks, where it expands as it freezes, gradually making them bigger. This is part of why timely sealing matters for smaller cracks.</p>
<h3><b>Can I seal cracks myself, or should I hire a professional?</b></h3>
<p>Small, simple cracks can sometimes be handled with DIY sealant products, but professionals are better equipped to tell whether a crack is surface-level or a sign of a bigger base problem underneath.</p>

<h2><b>Dealing With Cracks in Your Driveway or Patio?</b></h2>
<p>If you’re noticing cracks that keep coming back, or you’re not sure whether sealing will actually solve the problem, Ridgewell Landscape and Design can take a look and give you an honest read on what’s going on underneath. We serve the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and surrounding communities. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> or<a href="/contact/"> contact us</a> to get it assessed properly.</p>

